const e=[{id:"sv-1-001",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Six Sigma is often described as a **metric**, a **methodology** and a **management philosophy**. Which statement describes Six Sigma as a **metric**?",options:["A structured, data-driven roadmap (DMAIC) for solving process problems","A way of running the business in which leaders choose projects from strategy and manage by fact","A belt structure of trained practitioners who lead improvement projects","A capability level of about 3.4 defects per million opportunities at six sigma"],answer:3,hint:"Metric = a number that rates performance; methodology = how you solve problems; philosophy = how the business is managed.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"As a **metric**, “sigma level” is a number that rates how often a process produces defects. At the six sigma level (with the 1.5σ long-term shift) that is about **3.4 DPMO**."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only option D gives a measurable performance level. DMAIC is the **methodology**; leading by strategy and fact is the **philosophy**; belts are **roles**, not a meaning of Six Sigma."}],final:"Six Sigma as a metric = 3.4 DPMO capability level → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This describes Six Sigma as a methodology (DMAIC), not as a metric.","This describes Six Sigma as a management philosophy, not as a metric.","Belts are roles and responsibilities (1.1.6), not one of the three meanings of Six Sigma.",""]},{id:"sv-1-002",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A number-porting team checks **2,000** porting requests. Each request has **3** defect opportunities (number, ID document, port date). The audit finds **136** defects in total. Using the conventional **1.5σ shift**, what is the approximate **sigma level** of the process?",options:["2.0σ","3.0σ","3.5σ","3.8σ"],answer:2,hint:"DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities) × 10⁶, then sigma level = Z(long-term yield) + 1.5 (one-tailed).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$D = 136$, $U = 2000$, $O = 3$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{U\\times O}\\times 10^{6}$$ and $$\\text{Sigma level}=Z_{LT}+1.5$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate DPMO",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{136}{2000\\times 3}\\times 10^{6}=\\dfrac{136}{6000}\\times 10^{6}=22{,}667$$"},{title:"Convert to a sigma level",body:"Long-term yield $= 1-0.022667 = 0.97733$, so $Z_{LT}=2.00$ (one tail). $$\\text{Sigma level}=Z_{LT}+1.5=2.00+1.5=3.50$$ The sigma table agrees: 3.5σ ↔ 22,750 DPMO."},{title:"Decide",body:"About **3.5σ**."}],final:"DPMO ≈ 22,667 → sigma level ≈ 3.5σ → answer **C**",whyWrong:["2.0σ is Z long-term only — the 1.5σ shift was not added.","3.0σ comes from leaving out the 3 opportunities (DPMO = 68,000 instead of 22,667).","","3.8σ uses a two-tailed Z (splitting the defect rate across both tails); defects here are one-sided, so use one tail."],trap:"Sigma level = Z_LT + 1.5 — add the shift once, and only after the one-tailed Z is found."},{id:"sv-1-003",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which company developed Six Sigma in the **1980s** as a way to measure and reduce defects, before General Electric made it famous in the 1990s?",options:["Motorola","Toyota","Ford","Western Electric"],answer:0,hint:"Six Sigma was born in electronics manufacturing in the mid-1980s (Bill Smith); GE scaled it under Jack Welch.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Six Sigma was developed at **Motorola** (engineer Bill Smith, mid-1980s) and later adopted company-wide by GE under Jack Welch."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Toyota is the home of the Toyota Production System (Lean). Ford is linked to the moving assembly line. Western Electric is where Shewhart developed control charts in the 1920s."}],final:"Six Sigma originated at Motorola → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Toyota developed the Toyota Production System — the root of Lean, not Six Sigma.","Ford is linked to mass production and the moving assembly line, not Six Sigma.","Western Electric (Bell Labs) is where Shewhart created control charts in the 1920s — an earlier milestone of quality, not Six Sigma."]},{id:"sv-1-004",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which set of deliverables BEST shows that the **Define** phase of a Lean Six Sigma project is complete?",options:["A signed project charter, a SIPOC of the process, and VOC translated into CTQs","A validated measurement system and a baseline capability for the CTQ","A list of root causes verified with data","A control plan with a response plan, handed to the process owner"],answer:0,hint:"Each DMAIC phase has its own tollgate deliverables: Define = what problem, for whom, how big, which process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Define answers “what is the problem, who is the customer, what matters to them, and which process is in scope?” Its deliverables are the **charter**, a high-level process map (**SIPOC**) and **CTQs** derived from VOC."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"MSA and baseline belong to **Measure**, verified root causes to **Analyze**, and the control plan to **Control**."}],final:"Charter + SIPOC + CTQs = Define deliverables → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","MSA and baseline capability are Measure-phase deliverables.","Verified root causes are the Analyze-phase deliverable.","The control plan and handover are Control-phase deliverables."]},{id:"sv-1-005",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt lists five project deliverables:

- **P** Verified root causes
- **Q** Signed charter with CTQs
- **R** Control plan handed to the process owner
- **S** Validated measurement system and baseline
- **T** Piloted solution with results

In which order are they normally produced?`,options:["Q → P → S → T → R","Q → S → T → P → R","Q → S → P → T → R","Q → S → P → R → T"],answer:2,hint:"Follow DMAIC: Define → Measure → Analyze → Improve → Control.",steps:[{title:"Map each deliverable to its phase",body:"Q = Define, S = Measure, P = Analyze, T = Improve, R = Control."},{title:"Put the phases in DMAIC order",body:"Define → Measure → Analyze → Improve → Control gives **Q → S → P → T → R**."},{title:"Check the look-alikes",body:"Each wrong option swaps one neighbouring pair: Measure↔Analyze, Analyze↔Improve or Improve↔Control."}],final:"Q → S → P → T → R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Swaps Measure and Analyze — root causes cannot be verified before the measurement system and baseline exist.","Swaps Analyze and Improve — a solution is piloted before the root causes are verified.","","Swaps Improve and Control — a control plan is written before any solution has been piloted."]},{id:"sv-1-006",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A contact-centre team writes: *Customer satisfaction = f(wait time, first-contact resolution, agent product knowledge)*. Which of the following is an **X** in this equation?",options:["Agent product knowledge","Customer satisfaction","The function f itself","The monthly satisfaction report"],answer:0,hint:"Y = f(X): Y is the output (effect); the X’s are the inputs or process factors (causes).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In $Y=f(X)$ the **Y** is the outcome we care about and the **X’s** are the inputs that drive it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Customer satisfaction is the Y. Wait time, first-contact resolution and agent product knowledge are X’s — so “agent product knowledge” is an X."}],final:"Agent product knowledge is an input (X) → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Customer satisfaction is the output — the Y, not an X.","f is the relationship between the X’s and Y, not an input.","A report of the Y is a way of measuring the output, not a driver of it."]},{id:"sv-1-007",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Monthly P2 SLA compliance (the Y) has been stuck at 78%. The service manager’s plan is to email the team every morning with yesterday’s SLA result and to ask supervisors to “push harder”. Based on **Y = f(X)**, what should the Black Belt recommend instead?",options:["Report the SLA result twice a day so the team reacts faster","Set a higher SLA target so the team aims higher","Add a final quality check before tickets are closed","Identify and control the few X’s (e.g. routing rules, staffing per hour) that drive the SLA result"],answer:3,hint:"To change Y you must change the X’s; watching or demanding a better Y does not change it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y is a result of the X’s. Monitoring the Y only tells you **what** happened; managing the vital few X’s changes **why** it happens."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Daily emails, higher targets and more inspection all focus on the Y. The Y = f(X) approach finds the inputs and process factors that drive SLA breaches and controls them."}],final:"Control the vital few X’s → answer **D**",whyWrong:["More frequent reporting still only watches the Y; it does not change any cause.","A higher target changes the goal, not the process inputs that produce the result.","Final inspection catches failed tickets after the fact; it does not improve the X’s that cause breaches.",""]},{id:"sv-1-008",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In the Y = f(X) strategy, the main way to improve a process is to tighten inspection of the output Y so that fewer defects reach the customer. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Y = f(X): the output is a function of the inputs — fix the X’s, not the Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y = f(X) says the output is determined by the inputs and process factors. Lasting improvement comes from finding and controlling the **X’s**."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"Inspecting the Y only sorts good from bad output after it is made. It adds cost and does not change the causes, so the statement is **False**."}],final:"Inspection of Y does not fix the X’s → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["Inspecting the Y treats symptoms; Y = f(X) directs effort at controlling the causes (X’s).",""]},{id:"sv-1-009",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"During Define, the Black Belt collects this statement: *“Finance requires the network-repair process to cut external contractor spend by 15% this financial year.”* Which voice does this statement represent?",options:["Voice of the Business (VOB)","Voice of the Customer (VOC)","Voice of the Employee (VOE)","Voice of the Process (VOP)"],answer:0,hint:"VOC = what external customers need; VOB = what the organisation needs (cost, profit, compliance); VOE = what staff need/experience.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"VOB covers the organisation’s own requirements: profit, cost, growth, risk and compliance."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A finance target to cut contractor spend is a business requirement — **VOB**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No customer or employee is speaking. VOP is what the process actually delivers (its data), not a requirement."}],final:"A finance cost target is VOB → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","VOC is the external customer’s need (e.g. faster repair), not a finance cost target.","VOE comes from employees (e.g. tools, workload); this is a finance requirement.","VOP is the actual performance data of the process, not a requirement placed on it."]},{id:"sv-1-010",phase:1,topic:"1.1.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are sources of the **Voice of the Customer** for a broadband provider EXCEPT:",options:["Complaint logs from the contact centre","Post-installation customer surveys","Focus groups with small-business subscribers","The annual EBITDA target set by the board"],answer:3,hint:"VOC comes from the people who receive the output; targets set by the organisation are VOB.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"VOC sources capture what customers say or do: complaints, surveys, interviews, focus groups, social media, churn reasons."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"An EBITDA target is set by the board — a **VOB** requirement, not a customer voice."}],final:"The EBITDA target is VOB, not VOC → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Complaint logs are a classic reactive VOC source.","Surveys are a classic proactive VOC source.","Focus groups gather VOC directly from a customer segment.",""]},{id:"sv-1-011",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A Black Belt project to reduce repeat field visits stalls because the field-operations manager will not release technicians to help collect data. Who is primarily responsible for **removing this roadblock**?",options:["The Master Black Belt","The Champion","The Process Owner","A Yellow Belt team member"],answer:1,hint:"The Champion sponsors the project, secures resources and removes organisational barriers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **Champion** (project sponsor) selects and backs the project, provides resources and clears organisational roadblocks."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Getting a line manager to release people is a resource and authority problem — the Champion’s job."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The MBB coaches on method; the Process Owner owns the process day to day; a Yellow Belt helps with tasks but has no authority over resources."}],final:"Resource roadblocks go to the Champion → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The Master Black Belt coaches and mentors on methods; it does not control line resources.","","The Process Owner owns and runs the process and later the control plan, but escalation of cross-team resource barriers is the Champion’s role.","A Yellow Belt supports the team with tasks and has no authority to release resources."]},{id:"sv-1-012",phase:1,topic:"1.1.6",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"After a Black Belt project closes, who is responsible for running the improved process day to day and for keeping the **control plan** alive?",options:["The Champion","The Black Belt","The Master Black Belt","The Process Owner"],answer:3,hint:"The person who owns the process owns its results and its controls after handover.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At the Control tollgate the project team hands the process and control plan to the **Process Owner**, who is accountable for sustaining the gain."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Champion sponsors; the Black Belt moves on to the next project; the MBB coaches the programme."}],final:"The Process Owner sustains the control plan → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The Champion sponsors and supports the project but does not run the process day to day.","The Black Belt leads the project and then hands over; ongoing ownership is not theirs.","The MBB coaches and trains belts; it does not own operational processes.",""]},{id:"sv-1-013",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt drafts this SIPOC for business broadband activation:

| Suppliers | Inputs | Process | Outputs | Customers |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Sales team; Credit bureau | Signed order form; Credit score; **Activated service** | Capture order → Check credit → Configure line → Activate → Confirm | Welcome SMS; First invoice | Business subscriber; Billing team |

Which entry is in the **wrong column**?`,options:["Credit bureau listed as a Supplier","Billing team listed as a Customer","Activated service listed as an Input","The Process column showing only five high-level steps"],answer:2,hint:"Inputs go into the process; outputs come out of it. Internal teams can be customers; SIPOC steps are high level (about 4–7).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In a SIPOC, **Inputs** are what the process consumes and **Outputs** are what it produces for customers."},{title:"Apply to the table",body:"An activated service is the result of the Activate step — it is an **Output**, not an Input."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A credit bureau supplies the credit score (Supplier ✓). The billing team receives activation data (internal Customer ✓). Five steps is the normal SIPOC level of detail ✓."}],final:"“Activated service” is an output placed under Inputs → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The credit bureau supplies the credit score input, so it is correctly a Supplier.","Internal teams that receive an output are valid customers in a SIPOC.","","A SIPOC process column is meant to be high level — roughly four to seven steps is correct."]},{id:"sv-1-014",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does a Black Belt agree clear **start and stop points** for the process during Define?",options:["So that the project can cover the whole end-to-end value chain","So that the team, data collection and improvements all focus on the same agreed part of the work","So that the root causes can be listed before data are collected","So that the solution can be chosen at the Define tollgate"],answer:1,hint:"Boundaries define scope: where the process begins (trigger) and ends (output delivered).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Process boundaries (start trigger, stop point) fix the **scope**. They keep data collection, baselines and improvement work aligned to one agreed slice of the work."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Boundaries usually *narrow* scope rather than stretch it end to end, and root causes and solutions come much later (Analyze, Improve)."}],final:"Boundaries keep the scope focused and consistent → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Boundaries are set to keep scope manageable; stretching to the whole value chain causes scope creep.","","Root causes are investigated in Analyze, after data exist — not a reason to set boundaries.","Solutions are chosen in Improve; jumping to a solution in Define is a classic error."]},{id:"sv-1-015",phase:1,topic:"1.2.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"For an incident-management process, which of the following is NOT an **input**?",options:["An alarm from the network monitoring system","A fault call from a customer","A confirmation sent to the customer that service is restored","Knowledge-base articles used by the engineer"],answer:2,hint:"Inputs enter or are used by the process; outputs are what the process delivers.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Inputs are triggers, information and resources that the process uses. Outputs are what it delivers to customers."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"The restoration confirmation is produced at the end of the process — an **output**."}],final:"The restoration confirmation is an output → answer **C**",whyWrong:["A monitoring alarm is a trigger input that starts the incident process.","A customer fault call is a trigger input.","","Knowledge articles are information inputs used during the process."]},{id:"sv-1-016",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A team builds a CTQ tree from the need *“I want my fault fixed quickly.”* Which item is a properly formed **CTQ**?",options:["P2 time to restore, from ticket opened to service restored, ≤ 4 hours","Fast restoration","Customers feel happy with the repair","Engineers should receive more training"],answer:0,hint:"CTQ tree: Need → Driver → CTQ. A CTQ is measurable, with an operational definition and a specification.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ turns a vague need into a **measurable** characteristic with a clear definition and a **specification**."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Only option A states what is measured, from when to when, and the limit (≤ 4 h)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Fast restoration” is a driver; “customers feel happy” is the need; “more training” is a solution (an X), not a requirement."}],final:"Measurable CTQ with definition and spec → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is a driver — it has no measure or specification.","This restates the customer need; it cannot be measured as written.","This is a proposed solution (an X), not a customer requirement."]},{id:"sv-1-017",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Mobile customers never mention in surveys that their bill shows the correct plan price, but they complain loudly and some leave when it is wrong. In the **Kano model**, what type of requirement is “correct plan price on the bill”?",options:["Performance (one-dimensional)","Delighter (attractive)","Indifferent","Basic (must-be)"],answer:3,hint:"Kano: basic needs are expected and unspoken — they cause dissatisfaction only when missing.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **basic (must-be)** requirement is taken for granted. Meeting it gives no extra satisfaction; missing it causes strong dissatisfaction."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Customers do not mention bill accuracy but react strongly when it fails — the signature of a must-be need."}],final:"Unspoken but painful when missing = basic → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Performance needs are spoken and satisfaction rises steadily with more (e.g. data speed).","Delighters are unexpected extras; their absence causes no complaint.","Indifferent features cause no reaction either way — here the reaction is strong.",""]},{id:"sv-1-018",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"A CTQ should be stated as a measurable characteristic with an operational definition and a specification (or defect definition), so that performance against it can be measured. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"If you cannot measure it against a limit, you cannot count defects or set a baseline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ is the measurable form of a customer need. It needs a clear measure, an operational definition and a specification so that defects can be counted."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"The statement describes a properly formed CTQ, so it is **True**."}],final:"Measurable + defined + specified = CTQ → **True** (answer **A**)",whyWrong:["","Without a measure and specification there is no defect definition, so no baseline or sigma level can be calculated."]},{id:"sv-1-019",phase:1,topic:"1.2.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A draft CTQ reads: *“Fibre installation completed within 10 working days.”* Two sites start the clock differently — one at order capture, the other when credit is approved. What must be added before a reliable baseline can be measured?",options:["A tighter limit based on current process capability","A Kano classification of the requirement","An operational definition stating exactly when the clock starts and stops and which system records it","The improvement solution the team expects to implement"],answer:2,hint:"An operational definition makes a measure repeatable: what, how, start/stop, data source.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A CTQ is only measurable if everyone measures it the same way. That is the job of the **operational definition**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The two sites start the clock at different events, so their data cannot be compared or pooled. Define the start event, stop event and data source first."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specifications come from the customer, not from capability; a Kano label does not fix the measurement; a solution is an Improve item."}],final:"Add an operational definition (start/stop/source) → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Specifications should reflect customer need (VOC), not what the process can currently do (VOP).","A Kano category describes the type of need; it does not make the measurement consistent.","","A solution belongs in Improve and does not help define the baseline measure."],trap:"Inconsistent clock-start events make any baseline meaningless — define the measure before collecting data."},{id:"sv-1-020",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A regional service team records these annual costs (OMR):

- Re-testing and re-configuring lines that failed pre-activation tests: **46,000**
- Re-keying orders rejected by the provisioning system: **14,000**
- SLA penalty credits paid to customers: **72,000**
- Handling complaint calls from affected customers: **22,000**
- Quality monitoring of repair calls: **30,000**
- Training for new engineers: **20,000**

Using COPQ = internal failure + external failure costs, what is the **COPQ**?`,options:["OMR 60,000","OMR 94,000","OMR 154,000","OMR 204,000"],answer:2,hint:"COPQ = internal failure + external failure. Prevention and appraisal are quality costs but not failure costs.",steps:[{title:"Classify each cost",body:"Internal failure (caught before the customer): re-testing and re-configuring failed lines (46,000) and re-keying rejected orders (14,000). External failure: SLA credits (72,000) and complaint handling (22,000). Appraisal: quality monitoring (30,000). Prevention: training (20,000)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$COPQ=C_{IF}+C_{EF}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$COPQ=(46{,}000+14{,}000)+(72{,}000+22{,}000)=60{,}000+94{,}000$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$COPQ=154{,}000$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"OMR 154,000 a year is the “size of the prize” for the business case. Most of it reaches customers: $$\\text{External share}=\\dfrac{94{,}000}{154{,}000}=0.61=61\\%$$"}],final:"COPQ = OMR 154,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["OMR 60,000 is the internal failure cost only — external failure was left out.","OMR 94,000 is the external failure cost only — internal failure was left out.","","OMR 204,000 is the total cost of quality — appraisal (30,000) and prevention (20,000) were wrongly added to COPQ."]},{id:"sv-1-021",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing unit has annual revenue of **OMR 2,400,000**. Its quality costs are: failure costs **OMR 312,000**, appraisal **OMR 48,000**, prevention **OMR 24,000**. The company defines COPQ as internal plus external failure cost. What is COPQ as a **percentage of revenue**?",options:["13.0%","15.0%","16.0%","81.3%"],answer:0,hint:"COPQ % of revenue = COPQ ÷ revenue × 100, with COPQ = failure costs only.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Failure costs = 312,000; appraisal = 48,000; prevention = 24,000; revenue = 2,400,000."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{COPQ}\\,\\%=\\dfrac{COPQ}{\\text{Revenue}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{COPQ}\\,\\%=\\dfrac{312{,}000}{2{,}400{,}000}\\times 100=0.13\\times 100=13.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Failures consume 13% of revenue — a strong case for improvement."}],final:"COPQ = 13.0% of revenue → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","15.0% adds appraisal to the failure costs (360,000 of revenue), which this company’s definition excludes.","16.0% is the total cost of quality (384,000) as a share of revenue, not COPQ.","81.3% divides failure costs by total quality cost (312,000 by 384,000) — the wrong denominator."]},{id:"sv-1-022",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A fixed-line operator lists these annual quality-related costs:

| Cost item | OMR |
|---|---|
| Repeat visit to a customer’s premises after a failed installation | 38,000 |
| Testing new routers on receipt from the supplier | 15,000 |
| Re-keying orders rejected before activation | 22,000 |
| Bill credits for SLA breaches | 51,000 |
| Process training for new agents | 12,000 |
| Handling complaints about wrong bills | 27,000 |
| FMEA workshop for a new product launch | 6,000 |

What is the total **external failure** cost?`,options:["OMR 78,000","OMR 116,000","OMR 138,000","OMR 153,000"],answer:1,hint:"External failure = defects found after the output reaches the customer; internal failure = found before delivery.",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"External failure (customer affected): repeat visit 38,000; SLA credits 51,000; complaint handling 27,000. Internal failure: re-keying 22,000. Appraisal: router testing 15,000. Prevention: training 12,000 and FMEA workshop 6,000."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$C_{EF}=\\sum \\text{costs of defects found by or affecting the customer}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$C_{EF}=38{,}000+51{,}000+27{,}000=116{,}000$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"External failure cost = **OMR 116,000**."}],final:"External failure = OMR 116,000 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["OMR 78,000 treats the repeat visit as internal failure — but the customer was already affected, so it is external.","","OMR 138,000 is all failure costs (COPQ) — the internal re-keying cost was added in.","OMR 153,000 adds the appraisal cost of router testing to all failure costs."],trap:"Ask “did the defect reach the customer?” — if yes, it is external failure even if the work looks like rework."},{id:"sv-1-023",phase:1,topic:"1.2.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following are **internal failure** costs EXCEPT:",options:["Credits paid to customers for late installations","Reworking orders rejected by the provisioning system before activation","Scrapping damaged SIM stock found in the warehouse","Re-testing a software release after it failed an internal test"],answer:0,hint:"Internal failure = defect found before the customer receives the output.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Internal failure costs arise from defects caught **inside** the organisation; external failure costs arise **after** the customer is affected."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"Credits for late installation are paid because the customer experienced the failure — an **external** failure cost."}],final:"Customer credits are external failure → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Rework before activation is caught internally — internal failure.","Scrap found in the warehouse never reaches the customer — internal failure.","Re-testing after a failed internal test is rework caught before release — internal failure."]},{id:"sv-1-024",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A service desk records **400** reopened tickets in one month, already sorted for a Pareto chart:

| Reopen reason | Count |
|---|---|
| Not actually fixed | 168 |
| Wrong information given | 92 |
| Customer unreachable at closure | 64 |
| Duplicate ticket | 44 |
| Other | 32 |

What is the **cumulative percentage** at the third bar?`,options:["16.0%","42.0%","65.0%","81.0%"],answer:3,hint:"Cum %ₖ = (sum of the first k sorted counts) ÷ grand total × 100.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Total = 400. First three counts: 168, 92, 64."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{k}=\\dfrac{\\sum_{i=1}^{k} f_i}{\\sum f}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{3}=\\dfrac{168+92+64}{400}\\times 100=\\dfrac{324}{400}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{3}=81.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Three of five reasons cause 81% of reopens — the vital few."}],final:"Cumulative % at bar 3 = 81.0% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["16.0% is the third bar’s own share (64 of 400), not the cumulative percentage.","42.0% is the first bar only (168 of 400).","65.0% stops at the second bar (260 of 400).",""]},{id:"sv-1-025",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:`Complaint counts for a mobile operator are listed **alphabetically**:

| Category | Count |
|---|---|
| Activation delay | 45 |
| Billing error | 210 |
| Coverage | 30 |
| Device fault | 75 |
| Porting | 120 |
| Roaming | 20 |

What is the **smallest number of categories** that together account for at least 80% of complaints?`,options:["1","2","3","5"],answer:2,hint:"Sort categories in descending order first, then accumulate percentages until the target is reached.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Total $=45+210+30+75+120+20=500$."},{title:"Sort descending",body:"Billing 210, Porting 120, Device 75, Activation 45, Coverage 30, Roaming 20."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{k}=\\dfrac{\\sum_{i=1}^{k} f_i}{\\sum f}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{2}=\\dfrac{210+120}{500}\\times 100=66\\%$$ $$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{3}=\\dfrac{210+120+75}{500}\\times 100=\\dfrac{405}{500}\\times 100=81\\%$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Three categories (Billing, Porting, Device) pass 80%."}],final:"3 categories reach 81% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["1 takes 80:20 literally (20% of six categories ≈ 1); Billing alone is only 42%.","2 stops at 66% — still below 80%.","","5 accumulates in the listed alphabetical order (72% after four, 96% after five) without sorting first."],trap:"Never accumulate before sorting — a Pareto line only makes sense on descending bars."},{id:"sv-1-026",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab produced this Pareto chart table for **480** customer complaints (small categories combined into “Other”):

\`\`\`
Pareto Chart of Complaint Reason

Reason      Billing  Network  Delivery  Porting  Other
Count           184      138        72       40     46
Percent        38.3     28.8      15.0      8.3    9.6
Cum %          38.3     67.1      82.1     90.4  100.0
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["Network accounts for 67.1% of all complaints","“Other” should be tackled before Porting because its count is higher","Delivery alone is the vital few, because its Cum % is above 80%","Billing, Network and Delivery together account for about 82% of complaints and should be the focus"],answer:3,hint:"Percent = each bar’s own share; Cum % = running total. “Other” is a grouping of small categories and stays last.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"The **Cum %** row gives the running total: 38.3 → 67.1 → **82.1** at Delivery."},{title:"Read",body:"Billing + Network + Delivery = 82.1% of complaints. These are the vital few."},{title:"Decide",body:"Focus the project on these three categories. “Other” is several small reasons lumped together, so it is not one target."}],final:"Top three ≈ 82% → focus there → answer **D**",whyWrong:["67.1% is the cumulative share of Billing + Network; Network alone is 28.8%.","“Other” is a group of many small categories, kept last by convention — not a single cause to tackle.","Cum % 82.1 is the running total of three bars; Delivery itself is only 15.0%.",""]},{id:"sv-1-027",phase:1,topic:"1.2.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt runs *Tally* on **500** fault codes before drawing a Pareto chart:

\`\`\`
Tally for Discrete Variables: Fault Code

Fault Code  Count  CumCnt  Percent  CumPct
F01            62      62    12.40    12.40
F02           148     210    29.60    42.00
F03            35     245     7.00    49.00
F04           205     450    41.00    90.00
F05            50     500    10.00   100.00
     N=       500
\`\`\`

On the Pareto chart, what will the **cumulative percentage** be at the **second** bar?`,options:["29.6%","41.0%","42.0%","70.6%"],answer:3,hint:"Tally lists codes in code order, not by size. Re-sort descending before reading cumulative %.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"The tally is in **code order**, so its CumPct column is not a Pareto cumulative."},{title:"Sort descending",body:"F04 205 (41.0%), F02 148 (29.6%), F01 62, F05 50, F03 35."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Cum}\\,\\%_{2}=\\dfrac{205+148}{500}\\times 100=\\dfrac{353}{500}\\times 100=70.6\\%$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Bar 2 cumulative = **70.6%**."}],final:"Cum % at bar 2 = 70.6% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["29.6% is F02’s own percentage — the second bar’s height, not the cumulative value.","41.0% is the first bar (F04) only.","42.00% is the CumPct of the second row of the tally, which is in code order, not Pareto order.",""],trap:"A tally’s CumPct is not a Pareto cumulative unless the rows are sorted by count."},{id:"sv-1-028",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"An audit of **800** installation work orders finds **120** defects. The defects sit on **90** of the orders. What is the **DPU**?",options:["0.11","0.15","0.85","6.67"],answer:1,hint:"DPU = total defects ÷ total units (count defects, not defective units).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$D = 120$ defects, $U = 800$ orders. (90 is the number of defective orders.)"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPU=\\dfrac{D}{U}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$DPU=\\dfrac{120}{800}=0.15$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"On average each work order carries 0.15 defects."}],final:"DPU = 0.15 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.11 divides defective orders (90) by units — that is the proportion defective, not DPU.","","0.85 is 1 − DPU, reported as if it were a yield.","6.67 inverts the formula (units divided by defects)."]},{id:"sv-1-029",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing team checks **1,250** customer bills. Each bill has **8** defect opportunities. The check finds **75** defects. What is the **DPMO**?",options:["0.0075","7,500","60,000","480,000"],answer:1,hint:"DPMO = defects ÷ (units × opportunities per unit) × 10⁶.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$D = 75$, $U = 1250$, $O = 8$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{D}{U\\times O}\\times 10^{6}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{75}{1250\\times 8}\\times 10^{6}=\\dfrac{75}{10{,}000}\\times 10^{6}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$DPMO=0.0075\\times 10^{6}=7{,}500$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Check: $\\dfrac{7500}{10{,}000}=0.75\\%$ of opportunities are defective."}],final:"DPMO = 7,500 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.0075 is DPO — the result was not multiplied by one million.","","60,000 leaves out the 8 opportunities (DPU × 10⁶).","480,000 multiplies by the opportunities instead of dividing by them."]},{id:"sv-1-030",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A SIM-activation step receives **600** requests. **510** are completed correctly the first time, **60** fail and are reworked successfully, and **30** are rejected and cancelled. What is the **first time yield (FTY)** of the step?",options:["15.0%","85.0%","89.5%","95.0%"],answer:1,hint:"FTY = units passing first time with no rework ÷ units entering the step.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Units in = 600; right first time = 510; reworked = 60; scrapped = 30."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$FTY=\\dfrac{\\text{units right first time}}{\\text{units entering}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$FTY=\\dfrac{510}{600}=0.85=85.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Reworked units do not count as first-time passes — they are the hidden factory."}],final:"FTY = 85.0% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["15.0% is the first-pass loss (1 − FTY), not the yield.","","89.5% divides by the units that eventually passed (570) instead of the units entering (600).","95.0% counts reworked units as passes (570 of 600) — that is final yield, not first time yield."]},{id:"sv-1-031",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A four-step order-to-activation process has first time yields of **97%, 92%, 95% and 99%**. What is the **rolled throughput yield (RTY)**?",options:["16.1%","83.9%","92.0%","95.8%"],answer:1,hint:"RTY = product of the step first time yields.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$FTY_1=0.97$, $FTY_2=0.92$, $FTY_3=0.95$, $FTY_4=0.99$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$RTY=FTY_1\\times FTY_2\\times FTY_3\\times FTY_4$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$RTY=0.97\\times 0.92\\times 0.95\\times 0.99=0.839=83.9\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Only about 84 in 100 orders pass every step right first time."}],final:"RTY = 83.9% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["16.1% is 1 − RTY — the share that needs rework somewhere, not the yield.","","92.0% is just the lowest step yield — a bottleneck view, not RTY.","95.8% averages the yields instead of multiplying them."]},{id:"sv-1-032",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"Network change records have a **DPU of 0.35**. Assuming defects follow a Poisson distribution, what proportion of change records is expected to have **zero defects**?",options:["29.5%","35.0%","65.0%","70.5%"],answer:3,hint:"Poisson zero-defect yield: Y = e^(−DPU).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$DPU = 0.35$."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$Y=e^{-DPU}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Y=e^{-0.35}=\\dfrac{1}{e^{0.35}}=\\dfrac{1}{1.4191}=0.7047=70.5\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"About 70.5% of records are defect-free. Note it is higher than 1 − DPU, because some records carry two or more defects."}],final:"Y = e^(−0.35) ≈ 70.5% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["29.5% is 1 − e^(−DPU) — the share with at least one defect.","35.0% reads DPU as if it were a percentage of records.","65.0% uses 1 − DPU, which assumes no record has more than one defect.",""],trap:"DPU is not a proportion — use e^(−DPU), not 1 − DPU."},{id:"sv-1-033",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A back-office team works one shift of **450 minutes**, with **30 minutes** of breaks. Customers submit **140** cases per day. What is the **takt time**?",options:["0.33 min per case","2.79 min per case","3.00 min per case","3.21 min per case"],answer:2,hint:"Takt = net available working time ÷ customer demand in the same period.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Shift = 450 min; breaks = 30 min; demand = 140 cases/day."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{\\text{net available time}}{\\text{customer demand}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=\\dfrac{450-30}{140}=\\dfrac{420}{140}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Takt}=3.00\\ \\text{min per case}$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"One case must be completed every 3 minutes to keep pace with demand."}],final:"Takt = 3.00 min per case → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.33 inverts the formula (demand divided by time) — that is a rate, not takt.","2.79 subtracts the breaks twice (390 instead of 420 minutes).","","3.21 uses gross shift time (450 minutes) without removing breaks."]},{id:"sv-1-034",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A team runs a binomial capability analysis on **5,000** orders, **120** of which were rejected:

\`\`\`
Binomial Process Capability Analysis: Rejected orders

Summary Stats (95.0% confidence)
%Defective:    2.40
Lower CI:      1.99
Upper CI:      2.86
Target:        0.00
PPM Def:      24000
Lower CI:     19937
Upper CI:     28630
Process Z:    1.9774
Lower CI:     1.9013
Upper CI:     2.0550
\`\`\`

Minitab’s Process Z is a long-term Z. Using the conventional 1.5σ shift, what **sigma level** should the team report?`,options:["0.48σ","1.98σ","2.40σ","3.48σ"],answer:3,hint:"Sigma level (short-term) = Process Z (long-term) + 1.5.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"The **Process Z** row: 1.9774 (long-term, from 2.40% defective)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Sigma level}=Z_{LT}+1.5$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\text{Sigma level}=1.9774+1.5=3.48$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"Report about **3.5σ** (24,000 PPM)."}],final:"Sigma level = 1.98 + 1.5 = 3.48σ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["0.48σ subtracts the 1.5σ shift instead of adding it.","1.98σ is the long-term Process Z reported without the 1.5σ shift.","2.40σ reads %Defective as if it were a sigma value.",""]},{id:"sv-1-035",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Poisson capability analysis of network change records gives:

\`\`\`
Poisson Capability Analysis: Defects

Summary Stats
Total units:      400
Total defects:     72
Mean DPU:      0.1800
Min DPU:       0.0000
Max DPU:       0.6000
Target DPU:    0.0000
\`\`\`

Each change record has **6** defect opportunities. What is the **DPMO**?`,options:["0.03","30,000","180,000","1,080,000"],answer:1,hint:"DPMO = DPU ÷ opportunities per unit × 10⁶.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Mean DPU = 0.18 (= 72 defects over 400 units)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{DPU}{O}\\times 10^{6}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$DPMO=\\dfrac{0.18}{6}\\times 10^{6}=0.03\\times 10^{6}=30{,}000$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"3% of opportunities are defective."}],final:"DPMO = 30,000 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["0.03 is DPO — not multiplied by one million.","","180,000 is DPU × 10⁶ — the 6 opportunities were ignored.","1,080,000 multiplies by the opportunities instead of dividing (and exceeds one million, which is impossible)."]},{id:"sv-1-036",phase:1,topic:"1.2.5",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A three-step order process has these results for one month:

| Step | Units in | Right first time | Reworked then passed | Scrapped |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Order entry | 1,000 | 920 | 60 | 20 |
| Credit check | 980 | 931 | 39 | 10 |
| Activation | 970 | 873 | 87 | 10 |

What is the **rolled throughput yield**?`,options:["78.7%","90.0%","92.3%","96.0%"],answer:0,hint:"FTY per step = right first time ÷ units in; RTY = product of the FTYs.",steps:[{title:"Compute each FTY",body:"$$FTY_1=\\dfrac{920}{1000}=0.92$$ $$FTY_2=\\dfrac{931}{980}=0.95$$ $$FTY_3=\\dfrac{873}{970}=0.90$$"},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$RTY=FTY_1\\times FTY_2\\times FTY_3$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$RTY=0.92\\times 0.95\\times 0.90=0.7866=78.7\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Only about 79% of orders flow through all three steps without any rework or scrap, although 96% eventually come out — the gap is the hidden factory."}],final:"RTY = 78.7% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","90.0% is the lowest single step FTY (Activation), not the rolled yield.","92.3% averages the three FTYs instead of multiplying them.","96.0% is final yield (960 good out of 1,000) — it counts reworked units as passes."],trap:"Final yield hides rework; RTY exposes it."},{id:"sv-1-037",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A deployment team scores four candidate projects (1–9, higher is better; for Ease, 9 = easiest). Weights: Customer impact **0.5**, Financial benefit **0.3**, Ease **0.2**.

| Project | Customer impact | Financial benefit | Ease |
|---|---|---|---|
| Repeat field visits | 7 | 9 | 3 |
| Billing disputes | 9 | 5 | 5 |
| Password resets | 3 | 3 | 9 |
| Porting delays | 6 | 8 | 7 |

Which project should be selected?`,options:["Repeat field visits","Porting delays","Password resets","Billing disputes"],answer:3,hint:"Weighted score = Σ(weight × score); choose the highest weighted total.",steps:[{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$S=\\sum_{j} w_j \\times s_j$$"},{title:"Calculate each weighted score",body:"Repeat visits: $0.5\\times 7+0.3\\times 9+0.2\\times 3=6.8$. Billing: $0.5\\times 9+0.3\\times 5+0.2\\times 5=7.0$. Password: $0.5\\times 3+0.3\\times 3+0.2\\times 9=4.2$. Porting: $0.5\\times 6+0.3\\times 8+0.2\\times 7=6.8$."},{title:"Decide",body:"Billing disputes has the highest weighted score (**7.0**)."}],final:"Billing disputes = 7.0 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Repeat field visits has the highest financial score (9) but only 6.8 weighted — one criterion was used instead of the weighted total.","Porting delays has the highest unweighted sum (21) — the weights were ignored.","Password resets is the easiest (9) but scores lowest overall (4.2).",""]},{id:"sv-1-038",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Which draft is the BEST **problem statement** for a project charter?",options:["Billing is bad and customers are unhappy, so we need a new billing system.","From January to June, 6.2% of postpaid bills were disputed versus a 2% target, costing about OMR 41,000 a month in credits and handling.","Reduce disputed bills to 2% by December by automating tariff checks.","The billing team does not check tariffs carefully enough, which causes disputes."],answer:1,hint:"A problem statement says what is wrong, where, since when, how big (vs target) and its impact — with no cause or solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A good problem statement is factual and measurable: **what**, **when**, **how much** versus target and the **impact** — with no blame, cause or solution."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Option B gives the time window, the metric (6.2% vs 2%) and the cost impact."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A is vague and jumps to a solution; C is a goal statement with a built-in solution; D assumes a root cause and blames a team."}],final:"Factual, measured, with impact and no cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Vague (“bad”, “unhappy”) with no data, and it jumps to a solution.","","This is a goal statement, and it embeds a solution (automation).","This assumes a root cause and blames people before any data analysis."]},{id:"sv-1-039",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the main purpose of the **in-scope / out-of-scope** section of a project charter?",options:["To list the root causes the team will verify","To record the budget approved for the solution","To set clear boundaries so the project stays focused and avoids scope creep","To name the team members and their roles"],answer:2,hint:"Scope = boundaries: what the project will and will not cover.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The scope section states which processes, products, locations or customer segments are **in** and **out**, so the team does not try to fix everything."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Root causes come in Analyze; budget and team members are other charter sections (business case and team)."}],final:"Scope sets boundaries and prevents scope creep → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Root causes are not known at chartering; they are verified in Analyze.","Financial information belongs in the business case, not the scope section.","","Team members and roles are a separate charter section."]},{id:"sv-1-040",phase:1,topic:"1.3.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"All of the following normally belong in a Lean Six Sigma **project charter** EXCEPT:",options:["The problem statement","The solution the team has decided to implement","The goal statement","The project scope"],answer:1,hint:"Charter = business case, problem, goal, scope, team, milestones — never a pre-chosen solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A charter frames the problem and the target. The solution is found later, in Improve, after root causes are verified."},{title:"Find the intruder",body:"A pre-decided solution has no place in the charter — it short-circuits DMAIC."}],final:"A pre-chosen solution is not a charter element → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The problem statement is a core charter element.","","The goal statement is a core charter element.","The scope is a core charter element."]},{id:"sv-1-041",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"The baseline repeat-call rate is **18%**. Benchmarking shows an entitlement of **6%**. The Champion asks for a goal that **closes 60% of the gap** between baseline and entitlement. What is the target repeat-call rate?",options:["6.0%","7.2%","10.8%","13.2%"],answer:2,hint:"Goal = baseline − (fraction of gap × (baseline − entitlement)).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Baseline = 18%; entitlement = 6%; close 60% of the gap."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Goal}=B-g\\times (B-E)$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Goal}=18-0.6\\times (18-6)=18-0.6\\times 12$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Goal}=18-7.2=10.8\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Target: reduce repeat calls from 18% to 10.8%."}],final:"Goal = 10.8% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["6.0% takes the entitlement itself as the goal (closing 100% of the gap).","7.2% cuts the baseline by 60% (18 × 0.4) instead of closing 60% of the gap.","","13.2% applies the 60% from the entitlement end (6 + 7.2), which closes only 40% of the gap."]},{id:"sv-1-042",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt summarises **120** incident resolution times. A histogram shows a long right tail.

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: ResTime (hours)

Variable   N  N*   Mean  SE Mean  StDev  Minimum    Q1  Median     Q3  Maximum
ResTime  120   0  18.60     2.31  25.30     0.50  4.20    9.80  21.50   168.00
\`\`\`

Which value is the BEST baseline for the **typical** resolution time in the charter?`,options:["9.8 hours","18.6 hours","25.3 hours","168.0 hours"],answer:0,hint:"For strongly skewed data the median represents the typical value better than the mean.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Mean = 18.6, Median = 9.8, StDev = 25.3, Max = 168."},{title:"Check the shape",body:"Mean ≫ median and a long right tail (max 168 h) show strong right skew. The mean is pulled up by a few very long incidents."},{title:"Decide",body:"Use the **median (9.8 h)** as the typical baseline, and track the tail separately (e.g. % over the SLA)."}],final:"Skewed data → median 9.8 h → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","18.6 h is the mean, inflated by a few extreme incidents in the right tail.","25.3 h is the standard deviation — a measure of spread, not a typical value.","168 h is the single worst incident (maximum), not a typical value."]},{id:"sv-1-043",phase:1,topic:"1.3.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A project’s **primary metric** is average handle time (AHT) in the contact centre. Which is the BEST **secondary (consequential) metric** to track alongside it?",options:["AHT reported weekly as well as monthly","Number of agents who attended the AHT workshop","Repeat-call rate within 7 days","Total project spend to date"],answer:2,hint:"A secondary metric guards against the primary metric improving at the expense of something else.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Secondary (consequential) metrics catch unwanted side effects of improving the primary metric."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cutting AHT can push agents to rush calls, so customers call back. The **repeat-call rate** would reveal that."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A weekly AHT is the same primary metric; workshop attendance is an activity count; project spend is a cost record, not a process consequence."}],final:"Repeat-call rate guards against rushed calls → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is the primary metric at a different frequency, not a secondary metric.","This counts an activity, not a consequence in the process.","","Project spend is tracked for the business case; it does not show a side effect on the process or customer."]},{id:"sv-1-044",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"An automation project costs **OMR 48,000** one-off. It saves **OMR 5,000 per month** but adds **OMR 1,000 per month** in licence costs. What is the **payback period**?",options:["0.08 months","8.0 months","9.6 months","12.0 months"],answer:3,hint:"Payback = one-off investment ÷ net benefit per period (savings − running costs).",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Investment = 48,000; savings = 5,000 per month; running cost = 1,000 per month."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Payback}=\\dfrac{\\text{Investment}}{\\text{Net benefit per month}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Payback}=\\dfrac{48{,}000}{5{,}000-1{,}000}=\\dfrac{48{,}000}{4{,}000}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Payback}=12.0\\ \\text{months}$$"}],final:"Payback = 12.0 months → answer **D**",whyWrong:["0.08 inverts the formula (net benefit divided by investment).","8.0 adds the licence cost to the savings (6,000 per month) instead of subtracting it.","9.6 ignores the licence cost (48,000 over 5,000).",""]},{id:"sv-1-045",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A project reports first-year benefits of **OMR 84,000** in hard savings and **OMR 24,000** in cost avoidance. The project cost **OMR 28,000**. Finance counts **only hard savings** in ROI. What is the **ROI**?",options:["66.7%","200.0%","285.7%","300.0%"],answer:1,hint:"ROI = (benefit − cost) ÷ cost × 100, using only the benefits finance recognises.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Hard savings = 84,000; cost avoidance = 24,000 (excluded); cost = 28,000."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$ROI=\\dfrac{\\text{Benefit}-\\text{Cost}}{\\text{Cost}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$ROI=\\dfrac{84{,}000-28{,}000}{28{,}000}\\times 100=\\dfrac{56{,}000}{28{,}000}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$ROI=2.0\\times 100=200.0\\%$$"}],final:"ROI = 200% → answer **B**",whyWrong:["66.7% divides the net benefit by the benefit (56,000 over 84,000) instead of by the cost.","","285.7% includes the cost avoidance (108,000 benefit), which finance excludes.","300.0% is the benefit-to-cost ratio (84,000 over 28,000) without subtracting the cost."]},{id:"sv-1-046",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt lists claimed annual benefits (OMR):

| Benefit | OMR |
|---|---|
| Contracted overtime payments eliminated (budget line reduced) | 36,000 |
| Two planned new hires no longer needed (not in current budget) | 40,000 |
| 1,200 agent hours freed and redeployed to sales calls | 14,000 |
| Third-party SMS licence cancelled | 9,000 |
| SLA penalty payments to customers reduced | 25,000 |

How much qualifies as **hard savings**?`,options:["OMR 36,000","OMR 45,000","OMR 70,000","OMR 110,000"],answer:2,hint:"Hard savings = verified reductions in actual spend versus the current budget or baseline; cost avoidance and freed capacity are soft.",steps:[{title:"Classify each row",body:"Hard (actual spend falls): overtime 36,000; SMS licence 9,000; SLA penalties 25,000. Soft: avoided future hires (cost avoidance) and redeployed hours (capacity, no cash reduction)."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Hard savings}=\\sum \\text{verified reductions in actual spend}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$36{,}000+9{,}000+25{,}000=70{,}000$$"}],final:"Hard savings = OMR 70,000 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["OMR 36,000 counts only the payroll line; the licence and penalty reductions are also real cash reductions.","OMR 45,000 leaves out the reduced SLA penalties, which are a real reduction in money paid out.","","OMR 110,000 adds the avoided hires — cost avoidance, which is a soft benefit."]},{id:"sv-1-047",phase:1,topic:"1.3.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A project cut the handling time of billing disputes. Pilot samples show:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Handling time (min)

Sample    N   Mean  StDev
Before  250  42.00   9.60
After   250  30.00   8.10
\`\`\`

The unit handles **18,000** disputes per year. Finance confirms that every minute saved removes contracted overtime costing **OMR 0.40 per minute**. What is the **annual hard saving**?`,options:["OMR 1,200","OMR 86,400","OMR 216,000","OMR 302,400"],answer:1,hint:"Annual saving = (mean before − mean after) × annual volume × cost per minute.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Mean before = 42.0 min; mean after = 30.0 min → 12 minutes saved per dispute."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$S=(\\bar{x}_{before}-\\bar{x}_{after})\\times V\\times c$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$S=(42-30)\\times 18{,}000\\times 0.40=12\\times 18{,}000\\times 0.40=86{,}400$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"OMR 86,400 per year, counted as hard because finance confirmed the overtime cash reduction."}],final:"Annual saving = OMR 86,400 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["OMR 1,200 uses the pilot sample size (250) instead of the annual volume.","","OMR 216,000 values the “after” mean (30 min) instead of the minutes saved.","OMR 302,400 values the “before” mean (42 min) instead of the minutes saved."]},{id:"sv-1-048",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A value stream walk of **40** orders gives:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Order-to-activation (hours)

Variable          N   Mean
ValueAdded       40   2.40
NonValueAdded    40  21.60
TotalLeadTime    40  24.00
\`\`\`

What is the **process cycle efficiency (PCE)**?`,options:["10.0%","11.1%","90.0%","1,000%"],answer:0,hint:"PCE = value-added time ÷ total lead time × 100.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Value-added = 2.40 h; total lead time = 24.00 h."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{\\text{VA time}}{\\text{Total lead time}}\\times 100$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$PCE=\\dfrac{2.40}{24.00}\\times 100=10.0\\%$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"90% of the lead time adds no value in the customer’s eyes — a clear Lean opportunity (mostly waiting)."}],final:"PCE = 10.0% → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","11.1% divides VA by non-value-added time (2.4 over 21.6) instead of by total lead time.","90.0% is the non-value-added share of lead time, not PCE.","1,000% inverts the ratio (lead time over VA time)."]},{id:"sv-1-049",phase:1,topic:"1.4.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A provisioning team has **450** orders in progress on average and completes **150** orders per week (5 working days). Using **Little’s Law**, what is the average lead time in working days?",options:["0.33 days","3 days","15 days","90 days"],answer:2,hint:"Lead time = WIP ÷ throughput — keep the time units consistent.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"WIP = 450 orders; throughput = 150 orders per week = 30 orders per working day."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$\\text{Lead time}=\\dfrac{\\text{WIP}}{\\text{Throughput}}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\text{Lead time}=\\dfrac{450}{30}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\text{Lead time}=15\\ \\text{working days}\\ (=3\\ \\text{weeks})$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"To shorten lead time, cut WIP or raise throughput."}],final:"Lead time = 15 working days → answer **C**",whyWrong:["0.33 inverts the formula (throughput divided by WIP).","3 is the answer in weeks, wrongly labelled as days.","","90 divides WIP by days per week (450 over 5) — throughput was not used."]},{id:"sv-1-050",phase:1,topic:"1.4.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which pair of ideas forms the two pillars of the **Toyota Production System**, developed by Taiichi Ohno and the root of Lean?",options:["Just-in-time and jidoka","DMAIC and DMADV","SPC and design of experiments","ISO 9001 and Total Quality Management"],answer:0,hint:"TPS: make only what is needed when it is needed, and stop at the first abnormality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"TPS rests on **just-in-time** (pull, flow, takt) and **jidoka** (automation with a human touch — stop and fix problems at once)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"DMAIC/DMADV are Six Sigma roadmaps; SPC and DOE are statistical tools; ISO 9001 and TQM are quality management frameworks."}],final:"JIT + jidoka → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","DMAIC and DMADV are Six Sigma roadmaps, not TPS pillars.","SPC (Shewhart) and DOE are statistical tools, not TPS pillars.","ISO 9001 and TQM are quality management frameworks, not TPS pillars."]},{id:"sv-1-051",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An enterprise order process has very few errors (about **300 DPMO**), but orders take **12 days** end to end and 92% of that time is waiting between teams. Which approach should the Black Belt lead with?",options:["Lean flow tools such as a value stream map to remove waiting and hand-offs","A designed experiment to reduce variation in order data quality","A Gage R&R study on the order-entry system","A hypothesis test comparing error rates between teams"],answer:0,hint:"Lean attacks waste and flow (speed); Six Sigma attacks variation and defects (precision).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Lean Six Sigma, Lean tools target **speed and waste**, and Six Sigma tools target **defects and variation**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Defects are already low, while lead time is dominated by waiting. This is a flow problem, so start with a **value stream map** and Lean waste removal."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"DOE, MSA and hypothesis tests are variation tools aimed at a defect problem the process does not really have."}],final:"Speed/waiting problem → Lean flow tools first → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A DOE attacks variation and defects, which are already low here.","A Gage R&R checks a measurement system; it does not address waiting time.","Comparing error rates targets defects, not the 92% waiting time."]},{id:"sv-1-052",phase:1,topic:"1.4.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Because Lean focuses on speed and waste while Six Sigma focuses on variation and defects, a Lean Six Sigma project must choose one of the two toolsets and cannot use both. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Lean Six Sigma integrates both: remove waste for speed, reduce variation for quality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lean and Six Sigma are complementary. Lean Six Sigma uses Lean tools inside the DMAIC structure wherever they help."},{title:"Apply to the statement",body:"A single project often uses both, e.g. a value stream map in Measure and a hypothesis test in Analyze. The statement is **False**."}],final:"The toolsets are combined, not exclusive → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["Lean Six Sigma deliberately combines the two toolsets; projects routinely use both.",""]},{id:"sv-1-053",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"Signed enterprise contracts are sent by courier between three offices so that each manager can sign in turn. Which of the seven wastes does this movement of documents represent?",options:["Motion","Waiting","Conveyance","Inventory"],answer:2,hint:"Conveyance (transport) = moving materials or information; Motion = unnecessary movement of people.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Conveyance** is the needless moving of materials, documents or information between locations."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The contracts (the work item) are being moved between offices — conveyance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Motion is about people moving at their workplace; waiting and inventory may follow, but the act described is moving the item."}],final:"Moving documents between sites = Conveyance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Motion is unnecessary movement of people (reaching, walking), not moving the item between sites.","Waiting may happen while the courier travels, but the waste described is the movement itself.","","Inventory is work piling up; the stem describes documents being transported."]},{id:"sv-1-054",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Every Monday the provisioning team prepares **200** router configurations for orders forecast for the week. By Friday about 60 are thrown away because orders changed or were cancelled. Which waste is the **root** of the problem?",options:["Overprocessing","Overproduction","Inventory","Correction"],answer:1,hint:"Overproduction = making more, or earlier, than the customer has demanded.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Overproduction** means producing ahead of, or beyond, actual demand. It creates other wastes such as inventory and correction."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Configurations are built to a forecast before any real order (push), so some are never needed."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The stock of unused configurations is inventory, and redoing them is correction, but both are **symptoms** of overproducing."}],final:"Building ahead of demand = Overproduction → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Overprocessing is doing more work on an item than the customer needs — not making items too early.","","The pile of unused configurations is inventory, but it is a symptom of overproducing.","Correction is fixing defects; discarded configurations here were not defective, just not needed."]},{id:"sv-1-055",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which example is **overprocessing**?",options:["Agents re-entering orders that the system rejected because of keying errors","Engineers waiting for change approval before starting work","Writing a four-page report for every low-priority P4 ticket that nobody reads","An engineer walking to another floor to collect a printer test page"],answer:2,hint:"Overprocessing = doing more than the customer requires or values.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Overprocessing** adds effort, steps or precision beyond what the customer needs or will pay for."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"A detailed report on every P4 ticket that no one reads is extra work with no customer value."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Re-entering rejected orders is correction; waiting for approval is waiting; walking to collect paper is motion."}],final:"Unneeded extra work = Overprocessing → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Re-entering rejected orders is rework — Correction, not overprocessing.","Idle time before work can start is Waiting.","","An engineer walking to fetch something is Motion."]},{id:"sv-1-056",phase:1,topic:"1.4.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which waste, if removed, typically reduces **inventory, conveyance and waiting** at the same time, and is therefore often called the most serious waste?",options:["Overproduction","Motion","Overprocessing","Correction"],answer:0,hint:"One waste creates the others: making too much, too early.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Making more or earlier than needed creates piles of WIP (inventory), which must be moved (conveyance) and sit idle (waiting)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Stopping **overproduction** removes the source of those downstream wastes."}],final:"Overproduction drives the other wastes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Motion concerns people’s movement and does not generate inventory or waiting downstream.","Overprocessing adds effort to each item but does not by itself create piles of inventory.","Correction (rework) is costly, but it is not the waste that creates excess inventory and transport."]},{id:"sv-1-057",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A network operations centre has run monthly 5S audits for a year. Scores were high in the first two months and then fell.

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: 5S audit score (0-5) by Pillar

Pillar        N  Mean  StDev  Minimum  Maximum
Sort         12  4.30   0.40     3.50     5.00
SetInOrder   12  4.10   0.50     3.00     5.00
Shine        12  3.90   0.60     3.00     5.00
Standardize  12  3.60   0.70     2.50     4.50
Sustain      12  2.20   0.90     1.00     4.00
\`\`\`

Which action BEST addresses the weakest pillar?`,options:["Run a new red-tag event to remove unneeded equipment","Install shadow boards and labelled locations for tools","Introduce a daily cleaning and inspection rota","Build 5S into leader standard work with regular audits, recognition and follow-up of gaps"],answer:3,hint:"Read the lowest mean; Sustain (self-discipline) is about habits, audits and leadership follow-up.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Lowest mean = **Sustain (2.20)**, with the widest spread."},{title:"Interpret",body:"High early scores that later fall is the classic Sustain failure: the first S’s were done once but did not become habit."},{title:"Decide",body:"Sustain needs discipline mechanisms — leader standard work, regular audits, recognition and follow-up."}],final:"Weakest = Sustain → build audit and leadership routines → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A red-tag event is a Sort activity — Sort already scores highest (4.30).","Shadow boards and labels are Set in Order, which scores 4.10.","A cleaning rota supports Shine (3.90), not the weakest pillar.",""]},{id:"sv-1-058",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:"In which order are the **5S** steps carried out?",options:["Straighten → Sort → Shine → Standardize → Self-discipline","Sort → Straighten → Shine → Standardize → Self-discipline","Sort → Straighten → Standardize → Shine → Self-discipline","Sort → Straighten → Shine → Self-discipline → Standardize"],answer:1,hint:"Remove what is not needed, give the rest a place, clean, make it the standard, then sustain it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"5S builds in order: **Sort** (remove unneeded) → **Straighten** / set in order (a place for everything) → **Shine** (clean and inspect) → **Standardize** (make it routine) → **Self-discipline** / sustain (keep it up)."},{title:"Check the look-alikes",body:"Each wrong option swaps one neighbouring pair."}],final:"Sort → Straighten → Shine → Standardize → Self-discipline → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Swaps Sort and Straighten — you would organise items that should have been removed.","","Swaps Shine and Standardize — you cannot standardise a condition that has not yet been created.","Swaps Standardize and Self-discipline — there is nothing to sustain until a standard exists."]},{id:"sv-1-059",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A team tags every cable, spare part and old device in the equipment room that has not been used in six months, and moves tagged items to a holding area for a decision. Which 5S step is this?",options:["Sort","Straighten","Shine","Standardize"],answer:0,hint:"Red-tagging separates what is needed from what is not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Sort** removes items that are not needed. The red-tag and holding-area method is its standard tool."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Straighten gives a home to what remains; Shine cleans and inspects; Standardize writes the rules down."}],final:"Red-tagging unused items = Sort → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Straighten (set in order) arranges the needed items; it comes after unneeded items are removed.","Shine is cleaning and inspection, not removing unused items.","Standardize documents the agreed state so the first three S’s are repeatable."]},{id:"sv-1-060",phase:1,topic:"1.4.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which activity belongs to the **Standardize** step of 5S?",options:["Writing visual standards and checklists so that sorting, ordering and cleaning are done the same way every time","Removing items that have not been used for months","Marking floor locations and outlining where each tool lives","Cleaning equipment and inspecting it for leaks or damage"],answer:0,hint:"Standardize = make the first three S’s routine with written and visual standards.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Standardize** turns the results of Sort, Straighten and Shine into agreed standards — checklists, visual controls, schedules — so they are repeated the same way."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Removing unused items is Sort; marking locations is Straighten; cleaning and inspecting is Shine."}],final:"Visual standards and checklists = Standardize → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Removing unused items is Sort.","Marking locations and outlining tools is Straighten (set in order).","Cleaning and inspecting is Shine."]}];export{e as items};
